## This is a GLASS HOUSE
It must read as enclosed in glass from every authored camera (judged: enclosed, not a diorama on
a flat plane), with the sun coming through the glazing and the frame throwing its shadows inside.
Name the roof / lid meshes `Ceiling…` / `RoomShell…` so the overview rig can lift them.
`roomShell({ glazed: true, frameColor, bay, openings })` from `lib/environment.js` builds a glazed
box on the room box you give it (the starter's `export const INTERIOR = { center, extents,
openings, glazed: true }` feeds it) — one option; a lean-to, a dome or a vault is yours to build.
Cut the plan's doors as `openings`.  What is seen through the glass must be something the brief
implies, never a void or a flat colour.
{% elif interior %}
## This is an INTERIOR
It must be enclosed — walls and a ceiling exist from every authored camera.  Measured 2026-09-07
over six interior runs: with no owner for walls and roof, every one was judged "not enclosed, a
diorama on a flat plane, tool racks floating at a missing wall" (0.0–0.3).  Name the lid meshes
`Ceiling…` / `RoomShell…` so the overview rig can lift them.  `roomShell({ center, extents,
openings })` from `lib/environment.js` is one way (a box on the room box you give it; the starter's
`export const INTERIOR = { center, extents, openings }` feeds it); a vault, a tent, a cave or an
irregular room is yours.  Cut the plan's windows and doors as openings (face, centre along the
wall / up from the floor, size, metres) and light the inside from the sources the brief names — a
sun rig stays outside, and a closed room with no openings and no practicals renders black and
fails the exposure gate.
